      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix: detect joined inline eval flags [AI]",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes an issue where users who allowlist interpreter or runtime binaries could have joined inline-code flags treated as ordinary interpreter invocations when strict inline-eval hardening was enabled.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe inline-eval detector now recognizes glued single-letter eval flags such as `-c...` and equals-joined long eval flags such as `--eval=...`, while keeping unrelated multi-letter flags exact-only unless their spec explicitly declares a prefix form. Regression coverage exercises the parser and the strict allow-always persistence boundary.\n\n## User Impact\n\nOperators using exec allowlists get the same inline-eval hardening for joined interpreter flag forms that already existed for separated flag/value forms. Normal script execution and unrelated options remain unchanged.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/infra/command-analysis/inline-eval.test.ts src/infra/exec-authorization-allowlist.test.ts` passed locally with 2 shards and 48 tests.\n- Remote validation was attempted first but unavailable in this environment: Blacksmith Testbox requires a newer Crabbox binary, and AWS Crabbox broker login is not configured.\n\nAI-assisted: yes.\n",
